BinaryEquals decodes request values, weakening policy denies
Link: https://chatgpt.com/codex/cloud/security/findings/890a5e9566fc819184f9e708a83e92da?sev=&repo=https%3A%2F%2Fgithub.com%2Fjustincormack%2Fargmin&sort=date_desc
Criticality: medium (attack path: medium)
Status: invalid

# Resolution
Reviewed against live AWS on 12/07/2026. The reported behavior is
AWS-compatible, so the finding is invalid for this repository's S3-compatibility
security model.

AWS evaluates `BinaryEquals` against base64-encoded request-context values for
`s3:RequestObjectTag/*`: an explicit Deny on
`BinaryEquals {"s3:RequestObjectTag/security":"cHVibGlj"}` denies a request tag
`security=cHVibGlj`, but it does not deny a raw request tag `security=public`
when a later Allow otherwise authorizes the request. Local behavior matches.

Regression coverage was added in
`test_bucket_policy_request_object_tag_binary_equals_deny_uses_base64_request_value`.
Validated with:

- `./scripts/aws-tests --test bucket_policy request_object_tag_binary_equals_deny_uses_base64_request_value -- --nocapture`
- `cargo nextest run -p s3-tests --test bucket_policy request_object_tag_binary_equals_deny_uses_base64_request_value`

Changing local policy evaluation to treat base64 policy operands as matching
raw request values would make Argmin diverge from AWS. Policies that intend to
reject raw tag strings should use AWS-compatible string conditions for those raw
values, or use `BinaryEquals` only with base64-encoded request values.

