# Summary
Introduced: BinaryEquals support is enabled for user-controlled S3 condition keys, but the matching routine incorrectly Base64-decodes the actual request value and treats non-Base64 actual values as NoMatch. This can weaken newly accepted BinaryEquals-based Deny policies.
The commit enables BinaryEquals for general evaluable condition keys, including attacker-controlled request tag keys such as s3:RequestObjectTag/* and s3:RequestObjectTagKeys. The evaluator then calls decode_binary_value(actual) on the request-context value and returns NoMatch if that value is not valid Base64. A bucket policy author using BinaryEquals with an explicit Deny to block a raw value represented by a Base64 policy operand can therefore be bypassed by sending the raw value. For example, a Deny on BinaryEquals {"s3:RequestObjectTag/security":"cHVibGlj"} intended to match the bytes/string "public" will not match a request tag security=public, because the implementation tries to Base64-decode "public" and treats the condition as NoMatch. If some other ACL, identity policy, or bucket policy statement otherwise permits the operation, the explicit Deny will not be enforced.

