## Report
Validated the finding against commit b20eff76e003b66cf260dca14730fde9294a3359. Code review showed that request tag condition keys are registered as OperatorSupport::AnyEvaluable at crates/auth/src/bucket_policy/condition_key.rs:151-174, and AnyEvaluable now accepts binary operators at condition_key.rs:747-753. The request tag resolver returns the raw PolicyTag value: condition_key.rs:362-366 calls request_object_tag_value, which reads tag.value directly at bucket_policy.rs:653-658. The BinaryEquals path decodes the actual request value as Base64: condition_op.rs:428-431 defines decode_binary_value, condition_op.rs:438-445 calls it on actual and returns false on decode failure, and condition_op.rs:448-455 converts that to ConditionMatchResult::NoMatch. For a Deny statement, NoMatch makes request_effect return None at bucket_policy.rs:812-815, after which a later Allow can win via bucket_policy.rs:98-105. I added a targeted integration PoC at crates/auth/tests/binary_equals_deny_bypass_poc.rs. It creates a policy with an explicit Deny on BinaryEquals {s3:RequestObjectTag/security: cHVibGlj} followed by an unconditional Allow. Running `cargo test -p auth --test binary_equals_deny_bypass_poc -- --nocapture` produced: encoded_actual_value_is_explicitly_denied ... ok; raw_public_value_bypasses_binaryequals_deny panicked with assertion left: ExplicitAllow right: ExplicitDeny. This demonstrates that security=public bypasses the intended Deny while security=cHVibGlj is denied. I also ran rust-lldb non-interactively on the failing test; the breakpoint at condition_op.rs:439 showed binary_value_matches(operands=["cHVibGlj"], actual="public") with a stack through eval_binary_equals -> condition_key::evaluate_clause -> PolicyStatement::request_effect -> BucketPolicy::evaluate -> the PoC test. gdb/rust-gdb was unavailable because gdb is not installed; valgrind is not installed; Rust ASan required nightly and nightly sync failed due network restrictions.

